  "articles": [
    {
      "title": "About the fidel",
      "body": "<p>The Ethiopic script, called <span lang=\"am\">ፊደል</span> (fidel), was first used for Ge'ez, the classical language of Ethiopia, more than 1,600 years ago. Ge'ez is still the language of the Ethiopian and Eritrean Orthodox churches. Today the script writes Amharic, Tigrinya, Tigre and several other languages of the Horn of Africa.</p>\n<p>It started as an abjad of consonants. Around the 4th century, vowels were added as small changes to each letter, which turned it into an abugida: every character is a consonant plus a vowel.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Reading the orders",
      "body": "<p>Charts of the fidel are laid out as a grid: one row per consonant, seven columns for the vowel orders ä, u, i, a, e, ə, o. Amharic has about 33 base letters, so the full grid has over 230 characters, plus extra forms with a w-glide (<span lang=\"am\">ሏ</span> lwa, <span lang=\"am\">ቋ</span> q'wa).</p>\n<p>You do not need to memorize 230 separate shapes. Learn the base letters, then learn the seven patterns. Most letters follow them; the ones that don't (<span lang=\"am\">ሉ</span>, <span lang=\"am\">ሩ</span>, <span lang=\"am\">ፉ</span>, <span lang=\"am\">ሎ</span> …) are worth noting as you meet them.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "The sixth order",
      "body": "<p>The sixth order (<span lang=\"am\">ል, ብ, ስ</span> …) is said with a short ə, or with no vowel at all. Amharic has no letter for a bare consonant, so the sixth order does both jobs: <span lang=\"am\">ልጅ</span> (child) is ləj, with the ጅ said as plain j.</p>\n<p>The rule of thumb: at the end of a word and before another consonant it is usually silent; between consonants that would be hard to say together it is a short ə.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Doubled consonants",
      "body": "<p>Amharic often doubles a consonant, holding it a little longer, and the fidel does not show it: <span lang=\"am\">ገና</span> is gäna (still, yet) and also gänna (Christmas). Doubling can change the meaning, so learn it with each word. Dictionaries and textbooks sometimes mark it with a dot or a line over the letter.</p>"
    }
  ]
}
